docBody.p:
You would think me very negligent in regard to the Newcastle Newspaper Article on poor Forster;William Lockey Harle, “John Forster: A Sketch,” Newcastle Daily Chronicle, 15 Feb. 1876. Harle (1801–1878), solicitor, alderman, and political reformer, had known Forster as a young man in Newcastle; he lived at 60 Victoria Sq., Newcastle. but the truth is I have been merely unfortunate & am in nothing to blame, the perfidious ElwinWhitwell Elwin (1816–1900; ODNB), rector of Booton, Norfolk, 1849–1900, old friend of Forster's; see 48:33TC to JAC, 25 Jan. 1872. John had written, 24 Feb.: “We shall all be glad to see that Newcastle account of dear Forster when you get it back from Mr Elwin.” instead of returning the paper to us that night carried it off to Norfolk & we have since heard nothing of him or of it. Saturday last, Mary finding Norfolk desperate wrote off to Newcastle requesting from the editor three copies; and sending the money withal with direction to send back the change. No answer came from the Newcastle editorJames Annand (1843–1906), Scottish journalist, editor of the Newcastle Daily Chronicle 1874–77. till this morning instead of three copies, there arrived eight with “change” retained;John replied, 3 March, that he too had written to Newcastle “asking for one or two copies, & received eight by return of post.” & singular to say, along with your copy which surprised me to see we possess in all nine copies at once,—and cannot do less than return you at least one of them in a clipped-out condition. The other 8 will probably find
owners by & by. The Newcastle lawyer might have told us more about the two Brothers father & Uncle of poor Forster and about his own Brother and two Sisters,Harle wrote: “John Forster's father was a butcher, and his uncle John Forster, usually termed ‘Gentleman John,’ was also a butcher. The uncle was a bachelor…. [He] spared no expense in procuring the best tuition for his favourite. The boy John had a brother, Christopher [1807–45], a merchant [marine insurance broker], and two sisters, Jane [1802–53], and Elizabeth [1818–68]. They were all Unitarians” (“John Forster: A Sketch”). Forster's father was Robert Forster (1777–1836), his mother, Mary, b. Angas (1777–1852), and his uncle, John Forster (b. 1771). of whom I distinctly, & with much respect, remember as probably you also do;Forster's sister Elizabeth had lived with the Forsters for the last months of her life in 1868; see 45:206TC to CPH, 1 Dec. 1868. John A. Carlyle had stayed with TC in London for two weeks on his way to Vichy, May 1868 (see 45:130TC to JCA, 18 May 1868), and probably again for a few days on his return in June (see 45:154TC to JCA, 30 June 1868), so he may have met Elizabeth Forster then. For her death, see 45:213–14TC to JF, 20 Dec. 1868. one emphatic word he says about Forster himself is very true, “his energy of purpose & strength of will were magnificent.” In fact his essential talent was what is called that of a man of business and distinguished itself & was capable of doing so still more as a man of action rather than of thought or speculation. Poor fellow his death looks more & more sombre and strange to me as the days pass on. I never had a kinder or more constantly helpful fellow-man in the world beside me. We have not seen poor Mrs Forster for a week; but hear that there are haggles about some details in his will especially about his magnificent Bequest to the S. Kensington Museum of all his Books, curious Manuscripts Autographs &c &c,In his will Forster bequeathed his collection of books, papers, manuscripts, paintings, and drawings to his wife, Eliza Forster, with the proviso that on her death it was to be donated to the Department of Science and Art at the South Kensington Museum (now the National Art Library at the Victoria and Albert Museum). which the absurd blockheads or at least their absurd President refuse to accept on the terms laid down.Sir Francis Philip Cunliffe-Owen (1828–94; ODNB), director of the South Kensington Museum 1874–93. Forster specified that the Museum set aside a separate room to house his collection, that it should never be sold, and that the collection be open to researchers. But doubtless the executors who are faithful clever people will soon put all to rights and relieve poor Mrs Forster from annoyance on that particular branch of the sad subject.In her desire to see her husband's wishes realized as soon as possible, Eliza Forster waived the bequest and gave the collection to the Museum at once. Forster's executors were Whitwell Elwin, Lord Lytton, and Joseph Chitty, but Lytton had just been appointed Viceroy in India, and while Chitty handled the legal business, the literary work fell on Elwin. Forster had stated that most of his personal papers and correspondence should be destroyed: “[Elwin's] own inclination, and Forster's instructions, were both in the direction of destroying all personal letters, however eminent the writer might be, but he nonetheless scrupulously examined everything, spending some months almost exclusively on the task” (Whitwell Elwin, Some XVIII. Century Men of Letters. Biographical Essays by the Rev. Whitwell Elwin … with a Memoir, ed. Warwick Elwin, 2 vols. [1902] 1:308). However, Eliza Forster refused to give Elwin full access to her husband's private diary and to hundreds of his personal letters; see Davies, John Forster 260–61, Richard Renton, John Forster and his Friendships (1912) 263–64, and Helena Langdon, John Forster as Biographer: A Case Study in Nineteenth Century Biography (Ph.D. Diss., Univ. Coll. London [2010]: 32). Poor soul, she looked very miserable the three times I have seen her;
but not quite so much the last time as the former two. I myself have a touching Bequest from Forster, his gold repeater Watch namely, which was a bequest to him from Dickens.Dickens had bequeathed the gold watch to Forster in his will, dated 12 May 1869: “I give my watch (the gold repeater presented to me at Coventry), and I give the chains and seals and all appendages I have worn with it, to my dear and trusty friend John Forster, of Palace Gate House, Kensington” (Forster, Life of Dickens [1874] 3:516). The watch had been given to Dickens by the Coventry Institute: “In December [1858] [Dickens] was entertained at a public dinner in Coventry on the occasion of receiving, by way of thanks for help rendered to their Institute, a gold repeater of special construction by the watchmakers of the town; as to which he kept faithfully his pledge to the givers, that it should be thenceforward the inseparable companion of his workings and wanderings, and reckon off the future labours of his days until he should have done with the measurement of time” (Forster, Life of Dickens 3:209–10). I have given it to Mary, and reposited safely a big gold chain that used to be worn along with it. This you will understand is a dead secret unknown to everybody except to us three and this is all I have to say about poor Forster for the present.

I was a little disappointed this morning and even yesterday by not hearing from you; but Froude's letter of this morning clearly enough explains it all;
and may entertain you a little to read.Froude was staying at Naworth Castle, Cumberland, with George James Howard and his family; Froude, Howard, and Howard's sister-in-law Maude Stanley had planned a trip to visit Craigenputtoch. Froude wrote, 7 Sept.: “Yesterday after being thrice disappointed by the weather George Howard, Maud Stanley and I went to Craigin Puttock…. The place in externals must be very much in the state in which you left it.” He described the house and surroundings in detail, and said that both Howard and Maude Stanley had made drawings. He continued: “We did not see your brother—we were obliged to watch for a suitable day & could not send him notice to be on the look out for us. Our time in Dumfries was too short to enable us to look for him.” John wrote, 19 Sept.: “Young James [Carlyle Jr.] was at Craigenputtock last week two days after Froude & his party had been there, & found that his hinds had been highly satisfied with the visit.” James Carlyle Jr. shared the tenancy of Craigenputtoch with his father but now lived at his own farm of Newlands, leaving Craigenputtoch to be managed by servants; see 49:193TC to AC, 4 March 1875. He appears not to have approached
Ecclefechan at all nor to intend doing so. I will without criticism leave him to his own course.
